<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sandžak</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Sanjak of Novi Pazar</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sandžak is a historical region on the border of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> and Montenegro, named after the Ottoman Sanjak of Novi Pazar. Its main towns include Novi Pazar, Pljevlja and Bijelo Polje, and much of its population is Bosniak Muslim. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a> garrisoned part of it from 1878 to 1908, and in 1912 it was divided between Serbia and Montenegro.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sandzak.jpg" alt="Sandžak" width="320" height="305" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: WikiEditor2004 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sandzak_region_map.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Sandžak covers around 8,500 square kilometres between the south-eastern border of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herzegovina">Herzegovina</a> and the borders with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosovo">Kosovo</a> and Albania. Six of its municipalities are in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> (Novi Pazar, Sjenica, Tutin, Prijepolje, Nova Varoš and Priboj) and seven in Montenegro (Pljevlja, Bijelo Polje, Berane, Petnjica, Rožaje, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gusinje">Gusinje</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plav">Plav</a>). The name is the Serbo-Croatian form of the Ottoman Turkish sancak, a province; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a> have historically known the region as Raška, of which it was part in the 12th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> invaded the Serbian Despotate in 1455. The spread of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> in Sandžak had several causes: Muslims did not pay the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jizya">jizya</a> tax or the child levy and were privileged over Christians in administration and the courts; after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-turkish-war">Great Turkish War</a> (1683–1699) part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> population was driven north and the land was settled by people who were or became Muslim; and the region was a centre of trade. Until 1790 it belonged to the Sanjak of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a>, which is why its Muslims identify with the Slavic Muslims of Bosnia.</p><p>When Serbian and Montenegrin troops seized the region in October 1912, many Slavic Muslims and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albanians">Albanians</a> left for Ottoman Turkey. In 1919 an Albanian revolt in the Rožaje, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gusinje">Gusinje</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plav">Plav</a> districts against inclusion in the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenes">Slovenes</a> was put down; seven hundred Albanians were killed in Rožaje and about 450 civilians in Plav and Gusinje.</p><p>In 1941 the region was partitioned between Italian-held Montenegro, the Italian protectorate of Albania and German-occupied Serbia, and Muslim militias, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chetniks">Chetniks</a> and Partisans fought there. On 2 March 1942 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> of Novi Pazar were rounded up and killed in camps. In May 1943 Chetnik forces under Pavle Đurišić massacred an estimated 5,400 Albanian men, women and children in Bihor, and an estimated 9,000 Muslims in all were killed by Chetniks and affiliated groups during the war. A Partisan council for Sandžak, founded at Pljevlja on 20 November 1943, accepted on 29 March 1945 the division of the region along the line of 1912.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The municipalities of Sandžak have around 361,656 inhabitants. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosniaks">Bosniaks</a> form 54.8 per cent, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a> 30 per cent, Montenegrins 5 per cent, people who declare themselves Muslims by ethnicity 3.4 per cent and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albanians">Albanians</a> 1 per cent. Novi Pazar is the economic and cultural centre. A referendum on autonomy organised by the Muslim National Council of Sandžak between 25 and 27 October 1991 was declared illegal by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>. The region remains underdeveloped, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> data show emigration by all its peoples.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sandžak” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the First Balkan War Montenegro besieged and took Scutari in 1913, but the powers forced it to hand the town to the new Albania; it did gain much of the <b>Sandžak</b> and Metohija, including Peć and Đakovica.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-montenegro">Kingdom of Montenegro</a></li><li><blockquote>A German offensive in late November 1941 retook Užice, and the Partisans withdrew into the <b>Sandžak</b> and eastern Bosnia, where they formed the First Proletarian Brigade.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-uzice">Republic of Užice</a></li></ul></section>
